{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoSaddleSumRule","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoSaddleSumRule","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Complex.Log","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Fin"],"declarations":45,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.pentagon_deriv_x_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.pentagon_deriv_x_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":853,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L853-L885","doc":"**The pentagon's `x`-derivative vanishes identically.**\n\nThe five terms are `L(x)`, `L(y)`, `L((1−x)/(1−xy))`, `L(1−xy)`,\n`L((1−y)/(1−xy))`, whose `x`-derivatives carry the chain-rule factors\n`1`, `0`, `(y−1)/(1−xy)²`, `−y`, `(1−y)·y/(1−xy)²`. With each composite\nlogarithm expanded into the five basic ones, the sum is zero — for **all**\nvalues of the five log variables, so the identity is independent of every\nbranch choice. Only the five non-vanishing conditions are needed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.rogersDeriv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.rogersDeriv","kind":"def","line":849,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L849-L851","doc":"`L′(z) = −½·( log(1−z)/z + log z/(1−z) )`, with the two logarithms passed in\nas free variables so the lemma makes no branch choice. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.nu_pi_part_eq_zero_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.nu_pi_part_eq_zero_iff","kind":"theorem","line":799,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L799-L818","doc":"**`ν = 0` forces `3q + 2p = 0`**, at a root where the branch integer is `0`.\n\nAt the real saddle root the branch relation reads `2·log y = 3·log(1−y)`, both\nlogs are real, and `ν`'s `πi`-part is `q·log y + p·log(1−y)`. Requiring it to\nvanish pins the flattening to the line `3q + 2p = 0` — which is exactly the null\ndirection Route 44 found by search and could only describe as a torsor. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.admissible_correction","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.admissible_correction","kind":"theorem","line":783,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L783-L797","doc":"**The admissible flattening correction is an integer multiple of `π²`.**\n\nNeumann's kernel condition forces `(p,q) = (3k, −2k)`. For such a flattening the\ncorrection term is exactly `k·π²·ε` — so in `ℂ/π²ℤ`, where `R` takes values, it\nvanishes whenever `k·ε` is an integer. That is why `R` is independent of the\nchoice of admissible flattening, and it is pure algebra over the branch\nrelation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.sum_pentagon_diagonal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.sum_pentagon_diagonal","kind":"theorem","line":744,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L744-L764","doc":"The same, summed over the three roots: a relation between **three** Galois\nsums rather than five.\n\nRoute 46 measures the right-hand side as `3π²/2` for the 5₂ saddle orbit with\nprincipal branches. Here it is a consequence of the pentagon hypothesis, with no\nbranch analysis of its own.\n\nThe hypothesis as stated — the pentagon for *all* `x, y : ℂ` — is false on the\ncuts, so it is not dischargeable in this form; that is why it is a hypothesis.\nThe true statements are `FiveTwoComplexPentagon.pentagonSum_eq_pi_sq_div_two`\n(regularity hypotheses) and `FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon`'s flattened pentagon\n(none). Neither is needed for the sum rule, which took the direct route. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.pentagon_diagonal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.pentagon_diagonal","kind":"theorem","line":726,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L726-L742","doc":"**The pentagon collapses to three terms on the diagonal.**\n\nWith `L` abstract and carrying the five-term (pentagon) relation, at `x = y = z`\n\n    2·L(z) + 2·L(1/(1+z)) + L(1−z²) = π²/2\n\nbecause `(1−z)/(1−z²) = 1/(1+z)` appears twice. `L` is abstract, so this is\nexactly as strong as the pentagon hypothesis and no stronger — no dilogarithm\ntheory is used or claimed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.pentagon_diagonal_arg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.pentagon_diagonal_arg","kind":"theorem","line":718,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L718-L724","doc":"On the diagonal, `(1−z)/(1−z²) = 1/(1+z)` — the collapse that makes two\npairs of pentagon arguments coincide. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.sum_V_eq_three_sum_flattenedRogers","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.sum_V_eq_three_sum_flattenedRogers","kind":"theorem","line":678,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L678-L704","doc":"**The sum rule is equivalent to the flattened statement**, summed over the\nthree roots.\n\n`Σ V(yⱼ) = 3·Σ R̂(yⱼ; εⱼ, 0) + (elementary)`. Since the correction contains no\n`Li`, proving either side's value proves the other's — which is exactly why the\nextended-Bloch reformulation is worth having rather than merely suggestive.\n\nWhen this was written it was a bridge between two open statements. It is now a\nbridge from a proved one: **the 5₂ sum rule is PROVED**, in\n`QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoComplexPentagon` (`fiveTwo_saddle_sum_rule`,\n`fiveTwoSaddleSumRule_holds`), sorry-free on the standard axioms. The flattened\nside is developed independently in `QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.lean`,\nwhere the pentagon holds with **no branch hypotheses at all** — which is the\nreason the flattening was worth having. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.V_eq_three_flattenedRogers_add_elementary","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.V_eq_three_flattenedRogers_add_elementary","kind":"theorem","line":653,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L653-L676","doc":"**The V-form and the flattened form differ by an elementary term.**\n\nFor each root, given only the branch relation `2·log y − 3·log(1−y) = 2πi·ε`,\n\n    3·Li(y) + (log y + b·πi)²\n      = 3·R̂(y; ε, 0) + (3πi/2)·ε·log(1−y) + πi·(ε + 2b)·log y − π²·b²\n\nwith the correction built from logs and the branch integers alone — no\ndilogarithm in it. The dilogarithm cancels identically; the content is the\n`log²` bookkeeping that the branch relation controls. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.flattenedRogers","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.flattenedRogers","kind":"def","line":645,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L645-L651","doc":"**The flattened Rogers dilogarithm**, with `Li` abstract.\n\n`p` multiplies `log(1−z)` and `q` multiplies `log z`, matching Neumann's\nconvention and the probes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.sum_distribution_of_duplication","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.sum_distribution_of_duplication","kind":"theorem","line":616,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L616-L622","doc":"The same for duplication on `w = u²`, giving `Σⱼ Li(1 − yⱼ) = 2·S₆`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.sum_distribution_of_triplication","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.sum_distribution_of_triplication","kind":"theorem","line":596,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L596-L614","doc":"**The distribution relation, summed over the orbit.**\n\nWith `Li` abstract and carrying only the triplication identity\n`Li (z³) = 3·(Li z + Li (ωz) + Li (ω²z))`, summing over the three cube-root\nparameters gives `Σⱼ Li(yⱼ) = 3·S₉`, with `S₉` the `Li`-sum over the nine\nnumbers `ω^k uⱼ`.\n\nOn its own this is one equation in one new unknown, and that is all this\ntheorem says — it is deliberately as weak as its hypothesis. Its value is that\n`saddle_comp_cube_factor` then splits `S₉` along a cubic and a sextic, so the\nnew unknown is not irreducibly new. Turning that split into a relation between\n`Li₂` sums needs the analytic input this file does not have. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.genPoly_reflected_comp_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.genPoly_reflected_comp_sq","kind":"theorem","line":591,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L591-L594","doc":"Generating-polynomial form of `reflected_comp_sq_factor`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.genPoly_saddle_comp_cube","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.genPoly_saddle_comp_cube","kind":"theorem","line":585,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L585-L589","doc":"Generating-polynomial form of `saddle_comp_cube_factor`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.cubeRoot_factor_neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.cubeRoot_factor_neg","kind":"theorem","line":572,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L572-L575","doc":"The two cubic factors of `reflected_comp_sq_factor` are exchanged by\n`X ↦ −X`, so the second orbit is the negation of the first. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.reflected_comp_sq_factor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.reflected_comp_sq_factor","kind":"theorem","line":566,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L566-L570","doc":"**`X⁶ − X⁴ + 2X² − 1` is reducible**, into *two* cubics — both of\ndiscriminant `−23`, i.e. both generating the same field as `y`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.saddle_comp_cube_factor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.saddle_comp_cube_factor","kind":"theorem","line":558,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L558-L564","doc":"**`X⁹ − 2X⁶ + 3X³ − 1` is reducible.** The nine cube roots of the saddle\norbit are not a single new Galois orbit: they split as a cubic and a sextic.\nThis is what makes the distribution relation informative rather than vacuous. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.branch_trivial_in_cubeRoot","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.branch_trivial_in_cubeRoot","kind":"theorem","line":550,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L550-L556","doc":"**The branch equation is trivial in the cube-root parameter.**\n\n`y² = (1−y)³` — the content of `cubic_iff_sq_eq_cube`, and the source of every\nbranch subtlety in this file — collapses to `u⁶ = u⁶` once `y = u³`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.pow_five_of_cubeRoot","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.pow_five_of_cubeRoot","kind":"theorem","line":544,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L544-L548","doc":"**`u⁵ = 1 − u`.** The reflection of the cube-root parameter is again a\npower of it — the plastic-number identity `ρ⁻¹ + ρ⁻⁵ = 1`, where `u = ρ⁻¹`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.reflected_cubic_of_cubeRoot","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.reflected_cubic_of_cubeRoot","kind":"theorem","line":538,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L538-L542","doc":"**`w = u²` solves `w³ − w² + 2w − 1`**, the minimal polynomial of `1 − y`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.saddle_cubic_of_cubeRoot","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.saddle_cubic_of_cubeRoot","kind":"theorem","line":530,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L530-L536","doc":"**`y = u³` solves the saddle cubic.** The cofactor is the sextic factor of\n`saddle_comp_cube_factor`, which is not a coincidence: it is the same\nfactorization seen from the other side. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.one_sub_cube_of_cubeRoot","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.one_sub_cube_of_cubeRoot","kind":"theorem","line":524,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L524-L528","doc":"`u³ + u² = 1` gives `1 − u³ = u²`: the cube-root parameter presents the\nsaddle root `y = u³` and its reflection `1 − y` at once. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.re_V_real_root_eq_rogers","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.re_V_real_root_eq_rogers","kind":"theorem","line":479,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L479-L490","doc":"**`Re V₃ = 3·L(y₃) − π²`**, with `L` the Rogers dilogarithm.\n\n`L₂` stands for `Li₂(y)`; the identity is about the logarithmic terms only, so\nno dilogarithm theory is needed and this file stays Mathlib-only. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.re_log_neg_sq_of_branch_relation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.re_log_neg_sq_of_branch_relation","kind":"theorem","line":468,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L468-L477","doc":"**The branch relation converts `Re Log(−y)²` into Rogers' correction.**\n\nGiven `2·log y = 3·log(1 − y)` — the real-root case of `y² = (1−y)³` — the\n`Log²` term of `V` is `(3/2)·log y·log(1−y) − π²`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.sum_re_log_neg_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.sum_re_log_neg_sq","kind":"theorem","line":409,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L409-L438","doc":"**The `Log²` half of the sum rule, evaluated.**\n\nFor the 5₂ saddle root configuration — one positive real root `y₃`, one\nconjugate pair `y₁`, `conj y₁` — with the Vieta relation `‖y₁‖² · y₃ = 1`:\n\n    Σⱼ Re Log(−yⱼ)² = (3/2)·log²y₃ − 2(θ − π)² − π²,   θ = arg y₁.\n\nThe hypothesis `hnorm` is exactly `real_root_mul_normSq` rearranged; it is what\neliminates `‖y₁‖` in favour of the single real algebraic number `y₃`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.re_log_neg_sq_conj_of_im_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.re_log_neg_sq_conj_of_im_pos","kind":"theorem","line":397,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L397-L407","doc":"The conjugate of an upper-half-plane point contributes the *same*\n`Re Log(−·)²`: `arg(conj z) = −arg z` there, and `(−θ + π)² = (θ − π)²`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.re_log_neg_sq_of_pos_real","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.re_log_neg_sq_of_pos_real","kind":"theorem","line":386,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L386-L395","doc":"`Re(Log(−x)²)` on the positive real axis. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.re_log_neg_sq_of_im_neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.re_log_neg_sq_of_im_neg","kind":"theorem","line":375,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L375-L384","doc":"`Re(Log(−z)²)` below the real axis. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.re_log_neg_sq_of_im_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.re_log_neg_sq_of_im_pos","kind":"theorem","line":364,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L364-L373","doc":"`Re(Log(−z)²)` above the real axis. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.branch_shift_eq_log_neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.branch_shift_eq_log_neg","kind":"theorem","line":321,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L321-L340","doc":"**The three branch shifts at once.** For a positive real `y₃` and a\nconjugate pair `y₁` (upper half-plane), `y₂ = conj y₁`, the shifts\n`(−1, +1, +1)` are exactly `Log(−yⱼ)`.\n\nThis is the formal statement that the sum rule's `b` carries no free\nparameters. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.log_neg_of_pos_real","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.log_neg_of_pos_real","kind":"theorem","line":309,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L309-L319","doc":"On the positive real axis, negation adds `πi` (`arg` of a negative real is\n`π`, not `−π`: the principal branch is closed from above). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.log_neg_of_im_neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.log_neg_of_im_neg","kind":"theorem","line":302,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L302-L307","doc":"Below the real axis, negation *adds* `πi`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.log_neg_of_im_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.log_neg_of_im_pos","kind":"theorem","line":295,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L295-L300","doc":"Above the real axis, negation *subtracts* `πi` from the principal log. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.FiveTwoDilogLadder.ladder","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.FiveTwoDilogLadder.ladder","kind":"theorem","line":271,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L271-L271","doc":"Some nontrivial linear relation over ℚ between `Σ Li(yⱼ)`,\n`Σ Li(1−yⱼ)` and `π²`, independent of the reflection identity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.FiveTwoDilogLadder.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.FiveTwoDilogLadder.mk","kind":"ctor","line":268,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L268-L268","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.FiveTwoDilogLadder","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.FiveTwoDilogLadder","kind":"class","line":244,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L244-L273","doc":"**CONJECTURE (unproved).** A second relation between `A` and `B`.\n\n`sum_reflection_of_branch` gives one equation in `A = Σ Li₂(yⱼ)` and\n`B = Σ Li₂(1−yⱼ)`. Numerically `A ≈ 0.9717306815` and `B ≈ 0.3514640301`,\nneither with an evident closed form.\n\nHistorical note: this class was introduced because a second independent relation\nlooked *necessary* to close the sum rule. It was not — the sum rule is now proved\nby the complex five-term route (`FiveTwoComplexPentagon`), which determines `A`\noutright rather than pinning it against `B`: what is proved there is\n`A = −π²/6 − ½·log²t + ⅔·(arg a − π)²`. Note also that the right-hand side of\n`sum_reflection_of_branch` is already explicit in the logarithms, so `B` follows\nfrom `A` by subtraction wherever that relation's reflection hypothesis holds —\nno *second* relation is involved in either direction. So this class is off the\ncritical path, and is retained because the Kubert route below is of independent\ninterest.\n\nThe identified route is the Kubert/distribution relation\n`Li₂(zⁿ) = n·Σ_{k<n} Li₂(ζₙᵏ z)` applied to `Li₂(y²) = Li₂((1−y)³)`, which\nsupplies one at the cost of cube-root-of-unity arguments `ω(1−y)`. That\nBloch-group computation is not formalized here.\n\nStated as a linear dependence between the two sums with coefficients\n`α, β, γ` to be identified; no instance is provided. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.sum_re_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.sum_re_eq","kind":"theorem","line":240,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L240-L240","doc":"`Σⱼ Re Vⱼ = −3π²/2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.mk","kind":"ctor","line":238,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L238-L238","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.FiveTwoSaddleSumRule","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.FiveTwoSaddleSumRule","kind":"class","line":214,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L214-L242","doc":"**PROVED.** The 5₂ saddle sum rule.\n\n`Vⱼ = 3·Li₂(yⱼ) + (log yⱼ + bⱼπi)²` over the three roots of the saddle cubic\nsatisfies `Σⱼ Re Vⱼ = −3π²/2`.\n\nThe witness is `QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoComplexPentagon.fiveTwoSaddleSumRule_holds`,\nwhich exhibits the roots and constructs this class at `Li = cLiTwoExt`,\n`y = ![a, ā, t]`, `b = ![-1, 1, 1]` — sorry-free, axioms\n`[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]`. The stronger closed form\n`Σ Li₂(yⱼ) = −π²/6 − ½·log²t + ⅔·(arg a − π)²` is\n`FiveTwoComplexPentagon.fiveTwo_saddle_sum_rule`, and the complex value\n`Σ Vⱼ = −3π²/2 + 2π·log t·i` is `fiveTwo_sum_V`.\n\nNo *global* instance is declared, and none should be: the class is parameterised\nby `Li`, `y`, `b`, and the statement is true at the 5₂ saddle data, not at\narbitrary arguments. That specificity was already visible numerically — five\ncontrol cubics of the same shape, four sharing the `Πy = 1` constraint, miss the\ntarget by 0.16 to 2.83 — so this is an arithmetic property of the 5₂ saddle\ncubic, not branch bookkeeping. Recorded residual before the proof: `3.4e-80` at\n80 dps.\n\nScope: this is the **sum rule**, not the 5₂ **volume** conjecture, which remains\nuntouched — the Bloch–Wigner `D` cancels identically on the Galois sum, which is\nexactly why the sum rule could be proved without reaching the volume. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.V_two_of_sum_rule","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.V_two_of_sum_rule","kind":"theorem","line":194,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L194-L204","doc":"**`V₃` is determined by `V₁`.**\n\nWith `V₂ = conj V₁` (hence equal real parts), the sum rule forces\n`V₃ = −2·V₁ − 3π²/2`. Consequently `V₃` never needs to be carried as an\nindependent numerical literal — which is what let the corpus keep a wrong\none (`−2.7580990292`) unnoticed for months. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.centering_constant_unique","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.centering_constant_unique","kind":"theorem","line":183,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L183-L192","doc":"**`π²/2` is the *unique* shift that centers the saddle values.**\n\nGiven the sum rule, `Σⱼ (Vⱼ + c) = 0` holds for exactly one `c`, namely\n`π²/2`. So the corpus's \"extraction convention\" — long treated as an\nunexplained fudge absorbed by a fit — is forced, not chosen. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.sum_reflection_of_branch","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.sum_reflection_of_branch","kind":"theorem","line":146,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L146-L176","doc":"**Summing the dilogarithm reflection identity over the three roots.**\n\nGiven the reflection identity `Li z + Li (1−z) = π²/6 − log z · log(1−z)` and\nthe branch data of the cubic — `2·log yⱼ − 3·log(1−yⱼ) = 2πi·εⱼ`, which is\nwhat `y² = (1−y)³` gives after taking logarithms — the `Li` values at `1−yⱼ`\nare eliminated in favour of log-squares:\n\n    Σ Li(yⱼ) + Σ Li(1−yⱼ)\n      = π²/2 − (2/3)·Σ(log yⱼ)² + (2πi/3)·Σ εⱼ·log yⱼ\n\nThis is **one equation in the two unknowns** `Σ Li(yⱼ)` and `Σ Li(1−yⱼ)`. It\ndoes not close the sum rule; see `FiveTwoDilogLadder`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.branch_data_of_cubic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.branch_data_of_cubic","kind":"theorem","line":125,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L125-L138","doc":"**The branch data of the 5₂ cubic, derived.**\n\nEvery root of `y² = (1−y)³` satisfies `2·log y − 3·log(1−y) = k·(2πi)` for some\ninteger `k`. Numerically `k = (+1, −1, 0)` at the three 5₂ roots, and this is\nthe sole source of the `π²` in the sum rule.\n\nThis discharges the `hbranch` hypothesis of `sum_reflection_of_branch` from the\ncubic alone — no dilogarithm, no analytic input. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.exp_branch_eq_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.exp_branch_eq_one","kind":"theorem","line":106,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L106-L123","doc":"`exp (2·log y − 3·log(1−y)) = 1` whenever `y² = (1−y)³`.\n\nThe exponential collapses the logarithms back to `y²/(1−y)³`, which the cubic\nmakes `1`. No branch choice is made here — that is the point. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.cubic_iff_sq_eq_cube","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.cubic_iff_sq_eq_cube","kind":"theorem","line":90,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSaddleSumRule.lean#L90-L98","doc":"The 5₂ saddle cubic **is** the branch equation `y² = (1−y)³`.\n\nBoth forms appear in the sources — GGM eq (233) at `x = 1` writes\n`y² = (1−y)³`, GGMW eq (184) writes `(1−ξ)³ = ξ²`, and the corpus carries\n`y³ − 2y² + 3y − 1`. They are the same equation, over any commutative ring:\nno positivity, ordering, or archimedean structure is used. ","signatureChunk":0}]}